<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- net9.0, matching blockcheck.csproj, tools/optioncheck and the CI job's
         setup-dotnet 9.0.x. -->
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
    <!-- Without this, a package whose types no source mentions is pruned from
         the output, and the blocks would then compile against a short list —
         which fails them for want of a reference rather than for want of
         correctness, believably and in the direction that confirms the thesis. -->
    <CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>true</CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- NO SOURCES, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THIS PROJECT EXISTING.

         Spec 016 friction 56: the probe's README documented a recipe nobody
         had run, and it took its references from the project that COMPILES the
         blocks. That project's build fails — the blocks do not compile, which
         is the whole premise — so bin/ holds 0 DLLs, and the run would have
         reported all 985 blocks broken. A believable number, in the direction
         that confirms the thesis. A project that compiles blocks cannot also
         assemble references for them.

         THE PIN LIVES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. Every entry carries an explicit
         version, so a version bump is this file and no other. blockcheck.csproj
         pins Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp, which is the compiler driving the
         check and not the API surface being checked — a different pin, in a
         different file, for a different reason.

         The list is the probe's, carried forward verbatim: 67 packages, which
         is the set the probe restored and ran against. Re-deriving it here
         would put a second, unproven list in the repository and the difference
         between the two would be silent. -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- THE REFERENCE SET IS WHAT MSBUILD RESOLVED, WRITTEN OUT.

       The probe took its references by copying `bin/*.dll` and then copying
       the framework targeting pack in beside them by hand:

           cp $(dirname $(which dotnet))/packs/Microsoft.NETCore.App.Ref/8.0.0/ref/net8.0/*.dll

       That works, and it is a documented step a person performs. Skipped, the
       run reports 981 blocks failing on CS0518 — "predefined type
       System.Object is not defined" — which is 99.9% of the corpus broken for
       want of a reference nobody mentioned. Measured, 2026-09-20, by skipping
       it: 233 assemblies, 1 block built, 984 failing.

       So the project states its own reference set instead. @(ReferencePath)
       after ResolveReferences is exactly what the compiler would have been
       given: the 67 packages AND the targeting pack for this TargetFramework,
       with no SDK version written down anywhere. tools/blockcheck reads this
       file, and refuses to run — exit 2, nothing checked — when it is absent
       or names an assembly that is not there. -->
  <Target Name="WriteReferenceList" AfterTargets="ResolveReferences">
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(OutputPath)refs.txt" Lines="@(ReferencePath)"
                      Overwrite="true" WriteOnlyWhenDifferent="true" />
  </Target>

